//! What Kumi can change in Logic Pro (M3, M4): a track's fader, pan and buttons, its name, tracks added, deleted and
//! put in Track Stacks, markers, the tempo and the Tempo and Signature Lists, a region's notes as Event List rows (or
//! many at once as a MIDI file imported), regions moved, split, resized, looped, renamed and deleted, the cycle range,
//! a plug-in's bypass, and a key command for what no other tool does; and (M4b) plug-ins put on a channel strip (a
//! track's or the Stereo Out's) and taken off it, their parameters set in their window's Controls View and their
//! settings stepped with its buttons, a patch from the Library, sends and their levels, Smart Controls, and automation
//! through the Track Automation Event List, with a bounce and an export as actions; and (M6) the metronome turned on
//! and off, and a sample loaded into Logic's Sampler on a new track. Each kind is the bridge's
//! (`crates/logic-kumi/src/changes`), which makes it through one macOS channel and reads it back; what the bridge found
//! no channel for (a marker's name, a track moved or coloured, a plug-in moved, a send's mode) isn't declared, and the
//! bridge refuses it saying why. Logic has no undo block, and its own undo keeps a step for an edit (a rename, a track, a
//! marker, a region, notes, a list's row, a stack, a plug-in inserted or removed, a patch loaded, a send made or
//! removed, an automation event, a sample loaded) and none for a header's control, the tempo, a bypass, the cycle, a
//! plug-in's parameter or setting (steps of the plug-in window's own undo), a Smart Control, a send's level or the
//! metronome: `plan_steps` is false, and
//! Kumi's undo puts every change back as a new write. The kinds Logic keeps no step for carry `skipped_by_daw_undo`, as
//! the host's `outside_undo` names them. Saving is the action `save_project` (the host's `project_save`), and a bounce
//! and an export (`bounce`, `export_tracks`) are actions too: files written, no change and no row.
use super::own_changes::{OwnAction, OwnChangeSet, OwnKind};
use crate::core::contracts::ChangeFamily::*;

/// (M6) The audio files Logic Pro imports (the bridge's `sample.rs`), by extension: what `sample_load` takes.
pub const LOGIC_AUDIO: &[&str] = &[".wav", ".wave", ".aif", ".aiff", ".aifc", ".caf", ".mp3", ".m4a"];

const PLAN: &str = "Make changes in Logic Pro in one call, in order: each step is one of your change tools or a step below with its input, and \"@name\" in an input stands for what an earlier step marked as: \"name\" made (a new track, a new marker, the right part of a split, an imported region, a Track Stack's track, a plug-in inserted, a send). A wait step ({\"beats\": 8} or {\"seconds\": 4}) pauses between steps. Logic Pro has no undo block: a step that renames, adds, deletes or stacks a track, makes, moves or deletes a marker, writes, imports or quantizes notes, moves, splits, resizes, loops, renames or deletes a region, makes or changes a tempo or a signature in its lists, inserts or removes a plug-in, loads a patch, makes or removes a send, adds, changes or deletes automation events, or runs a key command Logic Pro keeps a step for, is one step of Logic Pro's own undo (Cmd-Z in Logic Pro takes back the last one only), and loading a sample is two (the import, then the conversion); a fader, pan, mute, solo, arm, tempo, bypass, cycle, metronome, plug-in parameter or setting, Smart Control or send level step is none, since Logic Pro's undo doesn't keep those. A step that adds, deletes, renames or stacks a track, flattens a Track Stack, loads a sample, or adds, deletes, moves, resizes, renames, splits or imports a region makes Logic Pro's refs new: name what it made with as and @name, and read again for the rest. It stops at the first step that fails and says what was done. With final: true and every step done, Kumi tells the producer what changed and the answer ends there, with no reply from you: use it when the changes complete the request, even a single change.";
const UNDO: &str = "Undo one of your changes from this session: pass its change id (such as c3), or \"last\" for the latest one. Kumi puts back exactly what that change set, as a new write in Logic Pro; it only works while nobody changed the same thing in Logic Pro since, and if so, say what happened (discard: true puts it back anyway, only when the producer asks). A deleted track or region, a split, an import, a flattened Track Stack and a patch loaded come back only through Logic Pro's own undo, while that step is still its latest, and a sample loaded goes that way too (its Sampler track and both regions); a plug-in removed comes back that way with its settings, else it is inserted again by its name with Logic Pro's defaults, and a send made goes that way with the aux Logic Pro made for it, else the aux stays. What a key command did only Logic Pro's own undo takes back. With final: true, when the undo is all that was asked, Kumi tells the producer and the answer ends there, with no reply from you.";
const INSTRUCTIONS: &str = "Change Logic Pro with your change tools, or several changes at once with make_changes, using the refs your reads gave. Logic Pro's own undo keeps a step for an edit (a rename, a track made or deleted, a marker made, moved or deleted, a Track Stack, a region moved, split, resized, looped, renamed or deleted, notes written, imported or quantized, a tempo or a signature in its lists, a plug-in inserted or removed, a patch loaded, a send made or removed, automation events, a sample loaded, a key command that edits) and none for a fader, pan, mute, solo, arm, tempo or bypass, nor for the cycle range, a plug-in's parameter or setting (steps of the plug-in window's own undo), a Smart Control, a send's level or the metronome (its Edit menu stays as it was); undo_change takes back any change of yours as a new write (Logic Pro's own undo is the producer's), except what a key command did, which only Logic Pro's own undo takes back. Kumi changes Logic Pro through its own window and reads each value back: a change Logic Pro didn't show is answered as uncertain, so read it before going on. Adding, deleting, renaming or stacking a track, flattening a Track Stack, loading a sample, and adding, deleting, moving, resizing, renaming, splitting or importing a region, make Logic Pro's refs new: read again before using older ones (a ref the change made comes back with it). Positions are positionBeats, beats (quarter notes) from the project's start; Logic Pro shows them as bars, and its playhead takes whole beats, so a split, a region's edges and an import's place go on a beat. A region is what sits on a track in the Tracks area; its startBeats and endBeats count from the project's start, and its notes' start from the region's start. Many notes at once go in with midi_import (Kumi writes a MIDI file, and Logic Pro imports it as a new region on a new software instrument track below the one named); notes_add is for a few notes in a region that exists. A sample goes into Logic Pro's Sampler with sample_load, by its audio file's whole path (find_sounds finds them): Logic Pro brings it in on an audio track and makes a new Sampler track of it, leaving the audio region muted. metronome_set turns the click on or off. key_command runs one of Logic Pro's key commands by its English name for what no other tool does, never one that opens a dialog or leaves the project (Open, Save, Save As, Bounce, Import, Export: save_project saves). The cycle range is the locators; a Track Stack gathers tracks under a track of its own. A plug-in goes into a channel strip's effect slots by the name Logic Pro's plug-in menu shows (fx_add), on a track or on the Stereo Out (its ref is in the observation's outputs); Logic Pro moves a plug-in only by dragging it, so to move one take it off and add it again where it should go. Its parameters are set only in its window's Controls View, which Kumi opens and closes itself: read them (param) for their names and texts, and give fx_param what the parameter should read (-6 for \"-6.0 dB\") or a normalized share of its slider. A send goes from a track to a bus by its number (1 to 64), and Logic Pro makes an aux for a bus nothing takes yet; a new send is at −∞ unless volumeDb is given, and its mode can't be read or chosen from outside. A patch (patch_load) replaces a track's whole channel strip from the Library (its plug-ins, sends and Smart Controls). Automation goes through the Track Automation Event List, a parameter at a time, each event on a whole beat with its value as the list's Val shows it, 0 to 127 (an automation read lists a track's). bounce writes a range of the project to an audio file (24-bit WAV by default) and export_tracks an audio file per track into a folder; neither is a change. Pan runs -1 (left) to 1 (right) here, though Logic Pro's strips show -64 to 63; the tempo takes whole BPM; Logic Pro names markers itself. The observation's kumiChanges lists your latest changes and where each stands (applied, undone, kept, unsure, or expired: Kumi can't undo it any more, and its note says who can); the producer can undo one in Logic Pro or in HISTORY without telling you, so go by kumiChanges and Logic Pro, not by memory.";

pub static CHANGES: OwnChangeSet = OwnChangeSet {
    kinds: &KINDS,
    actions: &ACTIONS,
    plan_description: PLAN,
    undo_description: UNDO,
    instructions: INSTRUCTIONS,
    plan_steps: false,
    most_track_adds: None,
};

static ACTIONS: [OwnAction; 6] = [
    OwnAction { tool: "play", method: "transport_play", description: "Start Logic Pro playing from the playhead.", schema: r#"{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"properties":{}}"#, core: true, playing: Some(true), title: "Playing" },
    OwnAction { tool: "stop", method: "transport_stop", description: "Stop Logic Pro's playback and recording.", schema: r#"{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"properties":{}}"#, core: true, playing: Some(false), title: "Stopped" },
    OwnAction { tool: "set_cursor", method: "cursor_set", description: "Move Logic Pro's playhead to positionBeats (beats from the project's start, to the bar and beat) or position (seconds at the project's tempo); playback follows while it plays.", schema: r#"{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"positionBeats":{"type":"number","minimum":0},"position":{"type":"number","minimum":0}}}"#, core: false, playing: None, title: "Moved the playhead" },
    // (M4) Saving writes the package and changes nothing in the project: no row in HISTORY. Logic keeps no versions (that
    // is REAPER's), so the host's `version` isn't offered.
    OwnAction { tool: "save_project", method: "project_save", description: "Save the Logic Pro project where it is (File > Save), or as path (a whole path to a .logicx package that isn't there yet; the project is that file from then on). A project never saved is saved only under path, and never over a file. No row in HISTORY: saving changes nothing in the project.", schema: r#"{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":4096}}}"#, core: false, playing: None, title: "Saved the project" },
    // (M4b) A bounce and an export write files and change nothing in the project: no row in HISTORY.
    OwnAction { tool: "bounce", method: "bounce", description: "Bounce Logic Pro's project from startBeats to endBeats (beats from the project's start) to an audio file at path, a whole path to a file that isn't there yet (.wav, or .aif for aiff24), in a folder that is (File > Bounce > Project or Section, Normalize off): what Logic Pro plays through the Stereo Out, every track that isn't muted. format is wav24 (24-bit WAV, the default), wav16, wav32f (32-bit float WAV) or aiff24. The answer says what the file holds (seconds, rate, channels, bits). No row in HISTORY: a bounce changes nothing in the project.", schema: r#"{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"required":["startBeats","endBeats","path"],"properties":{"startBeats":{"type":"number","minimum":0},"endBeats":{"type":"number","minimum":0},"path":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":4096},"format":{"type":"string","enum":["wav24","wav16","wav32f","aiff24"]}}}"#, core: false, playing: None, title: "Bounced the project" },
    OwnAction { tool: "export_tracks", method: "export_tracks", description: "Export every track of Logic Pro's project as an audio file of its own into the folder path, whole and there already (File > Export > All Tracks as Audio Files, with the options the producer set in Logic Pro); the answer lists the files Logic Pro wrote there. No row in HISTORY: an export changes nothing in the project.", schema: r#"{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":4096,"description":"The folder the files go in"}}}"#, core: false, playing: None, title: "Exported the tracks" },
];

// The tools on every request (at most eight, as every own DAW's), REAPER's own eight: the fader, pan and mute, a track
